stringPointerT -- kant's string-pointer operations (Tony, 2026-11-21, SEQ 735; the pins moved at SEQ 737 under Tony's rulings
of 2026-11-22). R1a: ++ on a one-character string leaves NO VALUE, never an empty string (kant has no empty strings). R1b: a
bare field's truth is unchanged (if x; is existence); a string walk tests for data, while s.datA; R2: numbeR (accessor 27) is
the code of the first character, as a count; read only. incant/utilities' hexToNumber uses them.
SP-1, SP-2  while s.datA; ++s;  over "ff9": three turns, ff9, f9, 9; the third step leaves no value, datA reads 0, and the loop
            ends by itself. (Until SEQ 737: while s; took five turns, an empty string, then the tag.)
SP-3        s.numbeR on "A" reads 65. (Until SEQ 737 numbeR was no accessor.)
SP-4        s + 2. Once "skip two characters"; today a string on the left joins (SEQ 347 R3): abcdef2.
SP-5        s - 4 drops four characters from the end: ab.
SP-6        s -= 2 drops two from the end, in place: abcd; the other strings are untouched (the anti-vacuity pair).
SP-7        ++ on a one-character string: one step leaves no value (its tag prints); the second refuses by name and ends the
            action; the caller goes on. (Until SEQ 737 the first step left an empty string and the third refused.)
SP-8        a write to numbeR refuses by name and ends the action; the string is unchanged (A).
SP-9        numbeR on a field with no string refuses by name, naming the field.
HX          hexToNumber: "ff" 255, "ef" 239, "2b" 43, "2d" 45; it walks a copy, so the strings handed in are untouched.
